# Third-party files are stored EXACTLY as they were downloaded.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the developer machine, so without this Git would
# rewrite the line endings of the vendored CSS and JS on checkout. Nothing would
# break — the repo still stores LF, so a Linux deploy gets the bytes that were
# fetched and verified — but a Windows working copy would hold a different file
# from the one collectstatic hashed on the server, which is a difference nobody
# wants to be debugging later.
#
# These are regenerated by scratchpad/vendor_assets.py, never hand-edited, so
# there is also nothing here for a line-ending convention to be helping with.
static/vendor/** -text

# Belt and braces: font and image payloads are binary whatever the path says.
# db.sqlite3 is here for the same reason and matters more: it is a COMMITTED
# database that the render-demo deploy actually serves, and `core.autocrlf` is
# true on the developer machine. Git auto-detects it as binary today because
# there is a NUL byte early in the file, which is luck rather than a guarantee.
db.sqlite3 binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
